%I A385186 #42 Aug 05 2026 00:58:17
%S A385186 2,6,12,20,30,42,56,110,132,156,182,272,306,342,380,506,552,812,870,
%T A385186 930,992,1332,1406,1640,1722,1806,1892,2162,2256,2756,2862,3422,3540,
%U A385186 3660,3782,4422,4556,4970,5112,5256,5402,6162,6320,6806,6972,7832,8010,9312,9506
%N A385186 Oblong numbers of the form k*(k + 1) such that at least one of k and k + 1 is prime.
%C A385186 This sequence is the union of A036689 and A036690. These two sequences have exactly one common term, namely 6.
%C A385186 Apart from the initial term 2, the terms occur in consecutive pairs p*(p - 1) = A036689(n) and p*(p + 1) = A036690(n), one pair for each prime p = A000040(n) >= 3; these are the two oblong numbers adjacent to the prime square p^2 = A001248(n).
%F A385186 a(1) = 2; for n >= 1, a(2*n) = A036689(n+1) and a(2*n+1) = A036690(n+1). - Formula adapted from _Joerg Arndt_'s suggestion.
%F A385186 Let K = floor((sqrt(4*x + 1) - 1)/2). For x >= 6, the number of terms not exceeding x is A000720(K) + A000720(K + 1) - 1. Consequently, the counting function is asymptotic to 4*sqrt(x)/log(x).
%F A385186 From _Amiram Eldar_, Aug 05 2026: (Start)
%F A385186 Sum_{n>=1} 1/a(n) = 2*Sum_{p prime} 1/(p^2-1) - 1/6 = 2*A154945 - A020793 = 0.936719928647331702212... .
%F A385186 Sum_{n>=1} (-1)^(n+1)/a(n) = 5/6 - 2*Sum_{primes p} 1/(p*(p^2-1)) = 10*A021016 - 2*A369632 = 0.390406590547741446484... . (End)
%e A385186 The prime 2 generates the two adjacent oblong numbers: 2 = 2*(2 - 1) and 6 = 2*(2 + 1).
%e A385186 The prime 3 generates: 6 = 3*(3 - 1) and 12 = 3*(3 + 1). Thus, 6 is generated twice, because 2 and 3 are the only two primes that are consecutive integers, and it occurs only once in the sequence.
%e A385186 The prime 5 generates: 20 = 5*(5 - 1) and 30 = 5*(5 + 1). The prime 7 generates: 42 = 7*(7 - 1) and 56 = 7*(7 + 1). And so on.
%e A385186 The first oblong number missing from the sequence is 72 = 8*9, because 8 and 9 are the first pair of consecutive positive integers neither of which is prime.
%t A385186 seq[max_] := With[{s = Select[Range[max], PrimeQ[ #] || PrimeQ[# + 1] &]}, s*(s+1)]; seq[100] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Jul 21 2026 *)
%o A385186 (Python)
%o A385186 from sympy import prime
%o A385186 def a(n): return 2 if n == 1 else (p:=prime(1+n//2))*(p-1+2*(n%2))
%o A385186 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 51)]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Jul 22 2026
%Y A385186 Cf. A000040, A000720, A001248, A036689, A036690. Subsequence of A002378.
